# RME invoice correction — pre-payment pilot VERIFICATION (rule 196)

Evidence: `docs/sprints/verification-rme-invoice-correction-prepayment-pilot-1.md`.
Extends rule 195 (activation contract). Rule 195 decides WHO may do WHAT; this rule
decides what counts as PROOF that the live pilot works and when it may be closed.
Nothing here changes runtime behaviour.

## 1. The state ladder — never collapse two rungs into one
Each rung needs its own evidence; a lower rung never implies a higher one.

| Rung | Means | Evidence (all of it) |
|---|---|---|
| `PILOT_READY` | the code can run the pilot | deployed runtime SHA, flag OFF→ON path proven, RoleSeeder grants match rule 195 |
| `PILOT_ACTIVE` | the flag is ON and the allowlist admits exactly the pilot branch | `rme:invoice-correction-readiness` → `STATE=PILOT_ACTIVE`, admitted codes `[SPN4]` |
| `PILOT_LIVE` | the maker and the checker are on shift in a shared pilot branch now | the probe `--maker=29 --checker=1` → `live_now=true`, Decision GO |
| `AUTHENTICATED_VERIFIED` | the two real operators logged in normally and saw/denied the right things | operator-performed login + working-branch selection, observed server-side (session/online-context rows, probe), never produced by anyone else |
| `FINANCIAL_CORRECTION_VERIFIED` | one real, owner-authorized correction went SUBMITTED → APPLIED and reconciled | correction + 2 events + 2 audit rows, v0 frozen, v1 issued, version +1, no other financial row changed |
| `FULL_ACTIVATION_GO` | the pilot is closed | every rung above + CI/rules/security/cleanup, then the full tag |

1. `PILOT_ACTIVE` with Decision WATCH is a legitimate resting state. It is not a
   failure and it is not a GO.
2. A verification run that cannot reach a rung reports the rung it reached and the
   missing evidence by name. It never rounds up.

## 2. What may and may not be done to reach a rung
3. **Never manufacture `PILOT_LIVE`.** The maker's working context exists only when
   the operator logs in. Never log in as them, never reset their password, never
   insert or edit a session / online-context / daily-context row, never open a REPL
   on production to "act as" them (rule 195 §28b–29).
4. **An online-context row's `status` column is not "on shift".** A row can read
   `status=online` days after the operator left — expiry is evaluated lazily from
   `last_seen_at`. Read the probe's `on_shift_in_pilot` / `live_now`, which applies
   the expiry without writing (a probe that writes the expiry is a defect — rule 165
   item 6a). Measured 2026-10-21: user 29's row read `online` with
   `last_seen_at` 2026-10-17; the probe correctly reported off shift and left the
   row untouched.
5. Run the probe ONLY as the runtime user (`runuser -u daengtisiams -- php artisan
   rme:invoice-correction-readiness --maker=29 --checker=1 [--json]`). It refuses root.

## 3. A real eligible case can only be caught — never created
6. An eligible case (rule 195 §23) exists only in the window between the cashier
   creating a POSITIVE invoice and the patient paying it. In SPN4 the cashier
   usually takes payment at handoff, so that window is short and a correction case
   arises only when an operator notices a genuine error inside it.
7. Therefore the full verification is OPPORTUNISTIC: when a real error is noticed
   before payment, the owner authorizes THAT case (invoice reference, reason,
   approved line changes, original and expected total, maker 29, checker 1), and
   only then does the maker submit and the checker approve.
8. Forbidden ways of producing a case, every one of them: creating an invoice with a
   deliberate error; delaying a patient's payment to keep an invoice UNPAID;
   editing an invoice's status or total; deleting or voiding a payment; using a
   synthetic patient or visit; correcting a historical Rp0 invoice (D21g PENDING —
   in 2026-10-21 the only UNPAID SPN4 invoice was Rp0, and it stays untouched);
   using a case that needs an undecided discount policy (D21c PENDING).
9. Generic approval to "finish the pilot" is never case-specific authorization for
   a particular patient's invoice. Missing case authorization →
   `CASE_SPECIFIC_AUTHORIZATION=PENDING`, no financial mutation.

## 4. Before and after any real correction
10. Capture a fresh read-only baseline immediately before (counts and sums of
    invoices, items, payments, payment batches, corrections, events, document
    versions, audit rows, role-permission pairs; the target invoice's version and
    total). Historical figures are never a substitute.
11. Reconcile after: exactly one correction APPLIED (`application_mode=AUTO_ON_APPROVAL`),
    maker ≠ checker, invoice `correction_version` +1, v0 unchanged, v1 matching the
    approved items and total, payment history unchanged, no other invoice changed.
    Any unexplained difference → `FINANCIAL_RECONCILIATION=FAIL`, stop, preserve
    evidence, follow the incident procedure. Never patch a financial row to make a
    reconciliation pass.
12. D10 is verified in production by STATE (the correction is SUBMITTED/APPROVED and
    the payment page shows the block), never by posting a real payment to see it
    refused. Payment-mutation behaviour is proven by the PostgreSQL suites.

## 5. Accounts (D1d) — a designation, not an allowlist
> **SUPERSEDED 2026-10-21 by owner decision D1e / rule 197.** The finding below is
> exactly why the owner narrowed the pilot: since
> `HARDENING-RME-INVOICE-CORRECTION-PILOT-IDENTITY-PAYMENT-ACTOR-1` the two D1d
> accounts ARE an enforced, source-controlled allowlist (maker 29 submits; checker
> 1 approves/rejects/recovery-applies; everyone else refused in the service). The
> measurement is kept as the historical record of what was true before.

13. D1d names the two accounts that perform the pilot ceremony. It is NOT an
    account allowlist: the grant reaches every Front Office account that can work
    in SPN4. Measured 2026-10-21 with the probe (`--user=` for every holder): 8
    active Front Office accounts (7, 8, 16, 17, 29, 30, 31, 32) + Super Admin 1 hold
    request/approve, and ALL 8 Front Office accounts are eligible for SPN4 — only
    user 29 is branch-pinned (to SPN4); 30/31/32 are not pinned in production, so
    the pin cohort is not a pilot boundary. Maker ≠ checker still holds per
    account. Narrowing the pilot to named accounts is a new owner decision,
    implemented in code — never by editing roles, pins or cohorts.
    Never infer an account's reach from a runbook's planned pin order; measure it.

## 6. Tags, deploys and closure
14. The pilot's full closure tag is `verification-rme-invoice-correction-prepayment-pilot-1-go`
    on the DEPLOYED runtime SHA. `activation-rme-invoice-correction-prepayment-2-go`
    is never created retroactively — one fact, one tag. Neither exists until
    `FINANCIAL_CORRECTION_VERIFIED` is reached.
15. A verification run that finds no runtime defect does NOT deploy: record
    `DEPLOY_REQUIRED=NO` / `NO_RUNTIME_DELTA` and verify the existing runtime. Never
    run a same-SHA redeploy to manufacture `DEPLOY OK`, never a meaningless commit to
    force CI, never a second scoped tag on a SHA that already carries one for the
    same evidence.
16. A defect found during verification follows the full lifecycle (failing test →
    minimal fix → security review → CI on the exact candidate → merge → deploy ON
    the VPS → production re-verification). Suspend the pilot first if the defect can
    move money wrongly: reject/cancel active corrections, flag OFF, config cache as
    `daengtisiams` (rule 195 §30).
17. Remaining FUTURE_SCOPE stays unreported as active: post-payment correction,
    refund, reversal, patient credit, Rp0 adjustment, discount authority, any branch
    other than SPN4.
